# Topic: Animals
T:Animals
T:Reference
T:Category
T:Science
T:------------
T:What it is
T:Animals are
T:multicellul,
T:eukaryotic
T:organisms
T:belonging to
T:the
T:biological
T:kingdom
T:Animalia.
T:Animals
T:consume
T:organic
T:material,
T:breathe
T:oxygen, have
T:muscle
T:cells, can
T:reproduce
T:sexually,
T:and grow
T:from a
T:hollow
T:sphere of
T:cells, the
T:blastula,
T:during
T:embryonic
T:development.
T:With few
T:exceptions,
T:animals are
T:able to
T:move.
T:Animals form
T:a clade,
T:meaning that
T:they arose
T:from a
T:single
T:common
T:ancestor.
T:Over 1.5
T:million
T:living
T:animal
T:species have
T:been
T:described,
T:of which
T:around 1.05
T:million are
T:insects,
T:over 85,000
T:are
T:molluscs,
T:and around
T:65,000 are
T:vertebrates.
T:It has been
T:estimated
T:there are as
T:many as 7.77
T:million
T:animal
T:species on
T:Earth.
T:Animal body
T:lengths
T:range from
T:8.5 μm
T:(0.00033 in)
T:to 33.6 m
T:(110 ft).
T:They have
T:complex
T:ecologies
T:and
T:interactions
T:with each
T:other and
T:their
T:environment,
T:forming
T:intricate
T:food webs.
T:The
T:scientific
T:study of
T:animals is
T:known as
T:zoology, and
T:the study of
T:animal
T:behaviour is
T:known as
T:ethology.
